  "interpretation": "# Usage interpretation\n\n## What changed\nOver 90 days, 3,904 people took 1,846,741 actions, and 58.02% came back more than once. Weekly users fell from 830 to 323 by late September, then rose to 1,204. In those quiet weeks, estimated work still grew, which suggests fewer people doing heavier tasks.\n\nIn the last 14 days, people rose 56.69% and actions rose 149.28%. Actions grew faster than people, so each person may be doing more. Release 2.6.11 reached 51.36% of users in the latest partial week.\n\n## How people use it\nReading and writing cell values and formulas lead. Tables and ranges hold 65.32% of actions. Share of work is an estimate from fixed effort levels: quick reads count 1, everyday edits 3, heavy data work 10. It is not time or speed. Tables and ranges get 54.9% of it, while macros rise from 6.91% of actions to 14.25% of work, and Power Query data imports from 3.29% to 8.87%.\n\nSince late September, command line users averaged 1,332.8 actions each, versus 254.7 for AI assistant users. These are different people over a shorter window, but command line users may focus more on tables and ranges (79.41% versus 60.02%).\n\nOver 30 days, a typical AI assistant session had 12 actions, and 70.62% used more than one area. Cells and sheets were paired most, in 59.39% of sessions. Of 3,095 new people, 47.92% returned after a week. Workdays averaged 41,453 actions, weekends 20,156. Most people tried advanced areas early: 80.42% of Power Query users started on their first day. Typical waits ranged from 0.12 to 1.19 seconds, depending on workbook size and computer.\n\n## Where people get it\nOver 12 months, npm counted 17,505 AI assistant server downloads and 8,303 command line downloads. Since each channel started, GitHub release files reached 31,910 and VS Code installs 10,015. Weekly npm downloads peaked at 7,459 in the week of October 25, the same week actions peaked.\n\nRelease 2.6.11 was downloaded most, with 1,119. GitHub and VS Code gained 3,285 in the week of October 26 and 1,862 in the latest week. Downloads count downloads, not people. One person can download several times, updates and automated installs add more, and channels overlap, so totals should not be added.\n\n## What we will improve\nThese are plans, not findings. We will make heavy data work, such as macros and Power Query, easier to follow. We will help new people return after their first week. We will make it easier to move between areas used together.",
  "interpretationModel": "GitHub Copilot CLI"
